    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Maiella Mountain located in the central Apennines, Italy, provides a unique reservoir analog case for characterizing fractures and faults in carbonate platform rocks in thrust belts. Excellent exposures in the Maiella region may allow establishing the temporal relationship between deformation mechanisms and the spatial relationship between the structures in the thrust sheet and the underlying fault. I propose to determine the orientation, density, temporal evolution and spatial distribution of joints, fractures, pressure solutions and faults first along a profile in coarse scale and later across the tilted limb and the flat top of the thrust fold overlying the fault in detail. In addition, I propose to analyze the change in behavior of deformation within differing lithologic units within the thrust sheet. Specific objectives of the proposed study are (1) to identify the processes of deformation and their structural products in terms of the tectonic environment related to the thrust geometry, slip magnitude, and the fold geometry (tilting of carbonate beds) and (2) to characterize the distribution and geometry of the major structural elements and their possible contribution to fluid flow.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
